Both runs validated their exact nonce marker and stopped at `retired=2,999,999,998`.
The series outcome is `passed`, but profile is `smoke_not_acceptance` and
acceptance is `diagnostic_not_counted`. Strict comparison is `matches=false`
with unexplained differences in `machine_observations` and
`ordered_uart_bytes` after nonce normalization. No comparator rule changed.
Each UART capture contains 23,356 bytes. Wall times were 1,257.737010666 s
and 1,244.194274334 s; `linux-direct` does not measure user CPU time.

Capture and joins
-----------------
Both sidecars are schema v8. Run 0001 recorded 43,871/43,871 entry
hits/samples and 33,994,592 selected poll hits with 66,395 stride-512 samples.
Run 0002 recorded 43,868/43,868 entry hits/samples and 33,991,205 selected
poll hits with 66,389 samples. Neither stream had omissions.

For each poll sample, the offline join searched entry samples for exact
`poll.start_tsc == entry.observed_tsc`, then required equal requested cycles,
RBP, and RSP. All 66,395 and 66,389 poll samples respectively had exactly one
timestamp match with matching context; there were zero unmatched timestamps,
ambiguous timestamps, or context mismatches.

Across the 43,868 shared entry ordinals, requested-cycle and RBP/RSP
sequences match exactly. CR3 differs at 33,847 shared ordinals. Consecutive
entry-TSC increments differ in 4,362 of 43,867 shared intervals. Causes and
relevance are unknown; these differences are not classified as benign.

Among the 35,787 shared entry ordinals that had at least one retained poll
sample, 643 calls have a different number of retained stride samples between
runs (322 with one extra in run 0001; 321 with one extra in run 0002). This is
only a difference in sparse sample coverage, not an exact per-call poll
count. Pairing the retained samples in per-entry order yields 66,068 shared
sample pairs; 321 paired records have different global poll-hit ordinals.
The sampled predicate `elapsed_cycles >= requested_cycles` differs in 68
paired records. The first is entry ordinal 10,207; both records are global
poll-hit ordinal 1,175,552 with request `0xf4217`, while elapsed values are
`0xf3631` and `0xf4219` (false/true). Sparse stride sampling does not locate
the first unsampled divergence or identify exact loop exit counts.

